      "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 18:47:53 GMT",
      "image": "https://kulu-media.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/abiy-ahmed-ethiopias-sea-access-tana-forum-2026-1000x600.jpg",
      "content": "Abiy Calls Ethiopia's Sea Access Existential at Tana Forum | Kulu Media Home Latest News Ethiopia Tigray Eritrea Sudan Africa World Politics Economy Finance Business Education More Analysis Entertainment Tech Health Sports English English ትግርኛ አማርኛ Connect with us Search Kulu Media Abiy Ahmed Calls Ethiopia’s Sea Access Existential, Offers Dialogue at Tana Forum Home Latest News Ethiopia Tigray Eritrea Sudan Africa World Politics Economy Finance Business Education More Analysis Entertainment Tech Health Sports English English ትግርኛ አማርኛ Home » News » Ethiopia » Abiy Ahmed Calls Ethiopia’s Sea Access Existential, Offers Dialogue at Tana Forum Ethiopia Abiy Ahmed Calls Ethiopia’s Sea Access Existential, Offers Dialogue at Tana Forum Abiy links Ethiopia’s maritime ambitions to security, sovereignty and regional cooperation. Published 3 hours ago on October 24, 2026 By Kulu Media News Desk Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed addresses the 12th Tana High-Level Forum in Bahir Dar on October 24, 2026, discussing Ethiopia's sea access, political dialogue and economic sovereignty. Photo Credit: EBC / YouTube, Screenshot by Kulu Media Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed has described reliable access to the sea as an existential issue for his country, while reaffirming his government’s willingness to pursue dialogue over political and security disputes. Addressing the 12th Tana High-Level Forum on Security in Africa in Bahir Dar, Abiy said Ethiopia’s population of more than 130 million made dependable international trade routes essential to its long-term economic development and national security. “For a nation of more than 130 million people, access to the sea is existential,” he told African leaders and delegates attending the forum. Abiy emphasized that Ethiopia would continue seeking solutions through dialogue and mutual benefit, framing maritime access as part of a broader strategy linking economic sovereignty, regional integration and lasting peace. His remarks came amid heightened tensions in the Horn of Africa, where Ethiopia’s pursuit of greater maritime access has become an increasingly sensitive diplomatic and security issue. Abiy Reaffirms Dialogue Amid Security Challenges The prime minister acknowledged the human consequences of conflict in Ethiopia, saying the effects of violence often extend far beyond the battlefield and continue to affect families long after fighting ends. He said protecting civilians and upholding the constitutional order remained central responsibilities of the government. At the same time, Abiy reiterated his willingness to engage groups prepared to abandon violence and pursue peaceful political dialogue. “We are ready and we will receive them with open arm and open heart,” he said. The remarks come as the Ethiopian National Defense Force (ENDF) and its Tigray Peace Force (TPF) allies continue military operations against TPLF-aligned forces in northern Ethiopia. Federal forces entered Mekelle earlier this month, and the TPLF subsequently confirmed its withdrawal from the regional capital. Abiy did not name the TPLF or specify particular armed organizations when extending his invitation to dialogue. The prime minister also discussed Ethiopia’s national dialogue process, saying recommendations had been submitted to parliament and regional governments. He called for those recommendations to be translated into concrete government decisions and practical measures. Reflecting on African-led mediation, Abiy argued that signing a peace agreement was only the beginning of a longer process requiring implementation, functioning institutions and improved livelihoods. His remarks come as the African Union continues efforts to preserve the peace process in northern Ethiopia, including a recent mediation initiative involving former Nigerian President Olusegun Obasanjo. Ethiopia’s Sea Access and Regional Trade Abiy placed particular emphasis on Ethiopia’s landlocked position, arguing that dependence on routes through neighboring countries created long-term economic and security vulnerabilities. He said Ethiopia would continue pursuing dependable access through negotiations and arrangements intended to benefit all parties. “This concerns our security, our sovereignty and the future of our children,” he said. The prime minister linked Ethiopia’s maritime ambitions to the development of regional transport corridors and implementation of the African Continental Free Trade Area. He argued that efficient cross-border transport could expand market access, support small businesses and reduce opportunities for illicit trade networks. His comments follow renewed regional debate over Red Sea security and maritime access. During a related discussion at the Tana Forum, Ethiopian National Security Adviser Redwan Hussein called for collective Red Sea security , emphasizing cooperation among coastal and landlocked countries. Abiy did not announce a new maritime agreement or identify a specific port arrangement in his speech. Energy Exports and the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam Turning to economic development, Abiy presented the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam as an example of Ethiopia’s efforts to strengthen domestic infrastructure and expand regional economic cooperation. He said the country’s investment in the dam should translate into more reliable electricity, industrial development and improved livelihoods. According to the prime minister, Ethiopian electricity is reaching Kenya, Sudan, Djibouti and Tanzania. He portrayed cross-border energy cooperation as a practical example of regional integration, arguing that shared infrastructure could strengthen economic ties and reduce tensions. The speech did not provide separate figures for electricity exports to each destination. Gold Revenue and Domestic Mineral Processing Abiy also highlighted Ethiopia’s mining industry, saying gold generated approximately $5.6 billion in revenue during the country’s last fiscal year. The figure was presented by the prime minister and was not independently verified in the speech. He said Ethiopia had historically exported much of its gold in raw form but was now seeking to capture more value through domestic processing. Abiy pointed to the opening of a precious metals refinery in Addis Ababa, which he said could serve Ethiopia and neighboring African countries by refining and certifying gold. He argued that African countries should expand mineral processing, technical training and transport infrastructure to create jobs and retain more revenue from their natural resources. The prime minister also called for greater participation by African businesses in investment projects across the continent. Digital Ethiopia 2030 and Artificial Intelligence Technology was another central theme of the address. Abiy said economic sovereignty increasingly depended on countries controlling their own digital infrastructure, data systems and technological standards. He highlighted Ethiopia’s Digital Ethiopia 2030 strategy, including efforts to connect the Fayda digital identification system with payment platforms and public-service data exchange. The prime minister said such systems could improve access to government services while emphasizing the importance of protecting personal information and providing mechanisms for correcting errors. He also announced plans to develop an artificial intelligence university intended to welcome students from across Africa. According to Abiy, the institution would focus on developing technologies capable of addressing practical challenges. The speech did not provide an opening date or detailed funding arrangements for the proposed university. Ethiopia Prepares to Host COP32 Abiy also connected environmental protection and climate adaptation to Africa’s long-term security. He warned that drought and environmental degradation could intensify competition over land and water, potentially contributing to conflict. The prime minister said Ethiopia had planted more than 56 billion seedlings over eight years through its Green Legacy Initiative. He presented the program as part of efforts to protect agricultural land, water resources and rural livelihoods. Abiy also reaffirmed that Ethiopia would host the COP32 United Nations climate conference in 2027. He invited African governments to coordinate their positions ahead of the gathering, particularly on climate adaptation financing and investment in clean energy. The prime minister said African countries should prioritize financing that reaches farmers and pastoralists while supporting energy projects that create jobs. Calls for Stronger African Institutions Abiy concluded by calling for stronger African institutions with adequate financing, professional staffing and broader representation. He questioned whether the African Union’s current staffing adequately reflected the continent’s diversity and argued that regional organizations needed resources and authority appropriate to their responsibilities. He said African governments should contribute more consistently to institutions responsible for addressing shared security and development challenges. Referring to Ethiopia’s traditional practice of elder-led mediation, known as shimglina , Abiy emphasized the importance of trust, reconciliation and keeping commitments. His address placed economic resilience, political dialogue and regional cooperation at the center of Ethiopia’s stated approach to peace and security. The speech also underscored the government’s position that access to the sea remains a strategic national priority, even as Addis Ababa continues to advocate negotiated solutions. The 12th Tana High-Level Forum has also featured calls for stronger African economic independence and regional security cooperation. Ghanaian President John Dramani Mahama urged African governments to strengthen economic sovereignty , expand domestic industries and finance their own peace operations. In a related Bahir Dar security dialogue, Ethiopian National Security Adviser Redwan Hussein called for collective Red Sea security , arguing that landlocked countries should also have a role in protecting regional maritime trade routes. Related Topics: Abiy Ahmed Economic Sovereignty Ethiopia Sea Access Red Sea Tana Forum Up Next Over 14,000 Ethiopians Flee Moyale Fighting Into Kenya Don't Miss Ghana’s President Calls for African Economic Sovereignty to Secure Lasting Peace Continue Reading You may like Ghana’s President Calls for African Economic Sovereignty to Secure Lasting Peace Getachew Reda Says TPLF Is No Longer Organized for Peace Talks, Sets Conditions for Eritrea Dialogue Ethiopia Calls for Collective Red Sea Security as Regional Tensions Rise Ethiopia Over 14,000 Ethiopians Flee Moyale Fighting Into Kenya Over 14,000 flee Moyale clashes as humanitarian needs surge in Kenya. Published 19 seconds ago on October 25, 2026 By Kulu Media News Desk Ethiopians who fled fighting in Moyale gather at a holding area in Kenya on October 23, 2026. Photo: Kenya Red Cross Society More than 14,000 Ethiopians have fled deadly fighting in the southern border town of Moyale into neighboring Kenya, as humanitarian agencies warn that shortages of food, clean water and sanitation facilities are threatening thousands of displaced families. The latest figures, reported on Saturday, October 24, 2026, mark a sharp escalation in the Moyale fighting displacement crisis, which began on October 21 amid tensions involving communities from Ethiopia’s Oromia and Somali regional states. According to Anadolu Agency , Kenya Red Cross officials said more than 10,900 displaced people were sheltering in six temporary camps in the Kenyan border town of Moyale, while approximately 4,000 others were staying with relatives and host families. The reported numbers represent a substantial increase from the 2,500 displaced Ethiopians documented in earlier reports on October 23. The growing number of arrivals has intensified pressure on humanitarian services in the Kenyan border region, where emergency responders are struggling to meet the needs of families fleeing the violence. Kenya Red Cross Warns of Critical Shortages The Kenya Red Cross has appealed for additional humanitarian assistance as displaced families face increasingly difficult living conditions. Malik Adan, the organization’s head of the Upper Eastern region, told reporters that emergency shelters urgently needed more food, drinking water, sanitation supplies and toilet facilities. Humanitarian officials warned that inadequate sanitation could increase the risk of diarrheal diseases, particularly in crowded temporary camps where access to clean water and hygiene facilities is limited. The Kenya Red Cross has distributed tents, blankets, sleeping mats and other essential supplies while providing nutritional assistance to children and breastfeeding mothers. Local authorities have also pledged additional food supplies, including rice and beans, but relief workers remain concerned that available resources may be insufficient for the growing displaced population. The latest displacement figures underscore the rapid deterioration of humanitarian conditions since fighting erupted earlier in the week. When the violence first intensified, Kenyan authorities reported that hundreds of Ethiopians had crossed the border, many seeking temporary shelter with relatives and local residents. The movement has since expanded into a much larger humanitarian emergency. Death Toll Feared to Rise as Fighting Continues The humanitarian crisis follows several days of deadly clashes on the Ethiopian side of Moyale. At least nine deaths were reported on October 23, according to hospital information cited in earlier reporting. Humanitarian officials now fear that the overall death toll may have exceeded a dozen, although an updated casualty figure has not been independently confirmed. Residents have reported continued gunfire and shelling in affected areas, raising concerns about further casualties and displacement. The fighting has been linked to tensions between Oromo and Somali communities in southern Ethiopia. According to Anadolu Agency, a dispute involving the display of the Somali Regional State flag in an area administered by Oromia contributed to the latest confrontation. However, the precise sequence of events that triggered the violence, the identities of all armed participants and responsibility for individual attacks remain incompletely verified. The fighting has also affected civilians on the Kenyan side of the border. Earlier reports documented a woman injured by a stray bullet in Kenyan Moyale, demonstrating how violence close to the international boundary can threaten communities in both countries. Cross-Border Trade and Local Communities Under Pressure Moyale is one of the most important border crossings connecting Ethiopia and Kenya. The town serves as a commercial and transportation link between the two countries, supporting cross-border trade and economic activity. Continued insecurity threatens to disrupt business operations, transportation and the movement of goods, potentially affecting communities whose livelihoods depend on the border economy. The arrival of thousands of displaced Ethiopians has also placed additional pressure on Kenyan host communities. While temporary camps accommodate most of those reported to have crossed the border, thousands are staying with relatives and local families. Such arrangements can provide immediate protection but may become increasingly difficult to sustain if the fighting continues and displaced families are unable to return home. The crisis has also created additional demands on local healthcare facilities, water supplies and emergency services. Humanitarian Response Faces Growing Challenges The rapid rise in displacement highlights the difficulty of responding to sudden population movements in areas where humanitarian resources are already limited. The Kenya Red Cross and local authorities are coordinating relief efforts, but additional assistance may be needed if more civilians cross the border. The emergency is unfolding against a wider backdrop of humanitarian funding challenges in the Horn of Africa. Earlier this month, the United Nations refugee agency warned that funding shortages had disrupted protection services for refugees in Ethiopia, including the suspension of registration affecting tens of thousands of Eritrean and South Sudanese refugees. That separate crisis illustrates broader pressure on humanitarian operations across the region, although it is not directly connected to the Moyale fighting. For families fleeing Moyale, the immediate concerns remain personal safety, temporary shelter, clean water, food and access to medical care. The latest displacement figures suggest that the consequences of the fighting have extended well beyond the original areas of confrontation. With the security situation still uncertain, humanitarian organizations face the challenge of assisting thousands of displaced people while preparing for the possibility of further arrivals. Whether the situation stabilizes will depend partly on efforts to end the violence and restore security in the affected border communities. Continue Reading Diplomacy Getachew Reda Says TPLF Is No Longer Organized for Peace Talks, Sets Conditions for Eritrea Dialogue Getachew questions TPLF’s negotiating capacity and outlines conditions for Eritrea talks. Published 12 hours ago on October 24, 2026 By Kulu Media News Desk Getachew Reda speaks during a panel discussion at the Tana Forum in Bahir Dar, Ethiopia, on October 23, 2026. Photo: ENA Getachew Reda has questioned whether the Tigray People’s Liberation Front (TPLF) remains sufficiently organized to participate in meaningful peace negotiations, while accusing Eritrea of interfering in Ethiopia’s internal affairs and outlining conditions for renewed dialogue between Addis Ababa and Asmara. Speaking during a panel discussion at the Tana High-Level Forum on Security in Africa, Getachew said the Ethiopian government remained willing to resolve disputes through dialogue but argued that military developments and divisions within the TPLF had complicated prospects for negotiations. His remarks addressed the renewed conflict in Tigray, the future of the Pretoria peace agreement, tensions between Ethiopia and Eritrea, and the wider security challenges facing the Horn of Africa. Getachew Questions TPLF’s Ability to Negotiate Getachew argued that the TPLF had lost much of its organizational capacity following recent military advances by the Ethiopian National Defense Force (ENDF) and allied forces. He claimed that federal troops had taken control of significant areas and that many senior TPLF leaders were either in Eritrea or hiding near the border. According to Getachew, the central question was no longer simply whether the government would agree to negotiations, but whether the TPLF could function as a credible negotiating party. He said he could not currently envision meaningful talks with the organization because, in his assessment, it lacked a unified leadership capable of representing its members and negotiating an agreement. His remarks contrast with a recent statement by TPLF chairman Debretsion Gebremichael, who vowed to continue fighting despite the loss of major towns. The claims come amid continued fighting between TPLF-aligned forces and the ENDF and its Tigray Peace Force (TPF) allies. Federal and allied forces entered Mekelle in early October, and the TPLF subsequently confirmed its withdrawal from the regional capital. Getachew’s assessment of the TPLF’s organizational condition could not be independently confirmed from his remarks alone. Three Conditions for Dialogue With Eritrea Turning to relations between Ethiopia and Eritrea, Getachew said Addis Ababa remained open to resolving disagreements peacefully but argued that meaningful negotiations would requ",
      "summary": "Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed emphasized the critical importance of maritime access for Ethiopia during his address at the 12th Tana High-Level Forum on Security in Africa, held in Bahir Dar. He described reliable sea access as an existential issue for the nation, which has a population exceeding 130 million, linking it to national security and economic development. Abiy reiterated his government's commitment to dialogue in resolving political and security disputes, inviting groups willing to abandon violence to engage in peaceful discussions. He highlighted Ethiopia's landlocked status as a vulnerability, advocating for negotiations to secure dependable trade routes. Abiy also discussed the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam's role in enhancing regional cooperation and energy exports. He called for stronger African institutions and emphasized the need for economic sovereignty and regional security collaboration. His remarks underscored Ethiopia's strategic focus on maritime access, regional integration, and the importance of addressing environmental challenges as part of a broader peace and security strategy.",
