      "content": "Somalia’s Health Minister Calls No-Confidence Motion a “Failed Coup” | Dawan Africa | Dawan Africa Sunday, October 25, 2026 Search Home Politics Business Opinion Sports Explainers Cryptocurrency Culture & Society Features News Tech Podcasts Videos Blockchain Somalia Kenya Djibouti Ethiopia Eritrea Somalia Kenya Djibouti Ethiopia Eritrea Loading article content... Uncovering the Continent — Through Its Own Lens 21 October Street, 405 Suldan Business Park, Mogadishu, Somalia +252628881171 Info@dawan.africa Quick Links Home Latest News About Us Categories Culture & Society Features Cryptocurrency Tech Blockchain Blockchain Recent Posts Ruto in Mwingi Pushes Roads, Housing and Investment Drive Oct 25, 2026 Opinion: In Somalia, Legitimacy Has Become a Matter of Who Wins Oct 25, 2026 South Sudan Opens 14 Oil Blocks, Courts Investors Ahead of SSOP 2026 Oct 25, 2026 Stay Updated with African News Get the latest breaking news and analysis delivered to your inbox. Subscribe Join our community of informed readers. Unsubscribe anytime. © 2026 Dawan Africa. All rights reserved. Privacy Policy Terms of Service Search Somalia’s Health Minister Calls No-Confidence Motion a “Failed Coup” October 25, 2026 2 min read BO By Bakar Osman - Reporter Mogadishu, 25 October 2026 – Somalia’s Minister of Health and Social Care, Dr. Ali Haji Adan, has described the no-confidence motion brought against him in the House of the People on Saturday as a failed “coup.” The minister made the remarks at a health sector networking event held in Mogadishu, hours after he survived the motion. “A small, failed coup was attempted to remove me from office,” Minister Ali Haji said. The minister said he was confident his work would be appreciated in the near future. “Thank God, in the next six months people will thank me,” he said. Related articles Ruto in Mwingi Pushes Roads, Housing and Investment Drive Opinion: In Somalia, Legitimacy Has Become a Matter of Who Wins The minister also said that when he was invited to the event, around the time of the motion, he asked the organisers whether they expected him to arrive in a sad mood. He said they told him they would pray for him and that he would come happy. “God accepted your prayers,” the minister said. The motion, brought before the House of the People, was signed by at least 20 MPs and submitted to the Speaker on 10 October. After the vote, 89 MPs rejected the motion, 50 supported it and 5 abstained, according to the Speaker of the House of the People, Abdulkadir Mohamed Nur. More articles for you South Sudan Opens 14 Oil Blocks, Courts Investors Ahead of SSOP 2026 Mogadishu Power Firms Raise Tariffs by 5 Cents Amid Fuel Price Surge Ad Ad Like ( 0 ) Save ( 0 ) Share More Articles Back to Top Related Articles Eritrea’s Information Minister Slams Ethiopia’s Red Sea Remarks at Tana Forum Oct 25, 2026 News Read more → Hormuud Telecom, UNDP Discuss Cooperation on Economic Development Oct 25, 2026 News Read more → Idman Bank Concludes Three-Month Financial Training for 30 Somali Women Entrepreneurs. Oct 25, 2026 News Read more →",
      "summary": "Somalia’s Health Minister, Dr. Ali Haji Adan, has labeled the recent no-confidence motion against him as a “failed coup.” Speaking at a health sector networking event in Mogadishu shortly after surviving the motion, he expressed confidence that his contributions would soon be recognized. The motion, initiated by at least 20 members of the House of the People, was submitted on October 10 and ultimately rejected by 89 MPs, with 50 in favor and 5 abstentions. Dr. Adan remarked on the support he received from event organizers, who prayed for his success, and he expressed gratitude for their prayers, stating, “God accepted your prayers.” He remains optimistic about his future in office, asserting that in six months, people will appreciate his work. The incident highlights ongoing political tensions within Somalia's government as it navigates challenges in governance and public health.",
