      "date": "Sun, 25 Oct 2026 08:41:16 GMT",
      "image": "https://ftlsomalia.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/SPeaker-Houe-of-the-People-U17-football.jpeg",
      "content": "Somali U-17 Squad Celebrated at Parliament as Calls for Sports Investment Grow - Somali News today Live Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Sports Nationwide Somali U-17 Squad Celebrated at Parliament as Calls for Sports Investment Grow October 25, 2026 5 min read MOGADISHU , Speaker of the House of the People Abdulkadir Mohamed Nur (Jaamac) hosted an evening of recognition and encouragement for Somalia’s Under-17 national football team on Saturday, honoring the players for their historic performance at the CECAFA U-17 Championship. According to the Speaker’s Office, the event celebrated the team’s achievement in reaching the final of the regional tournament, where they ultimately fell to Uganda on penalties. But the Speaker’s message was one of pride rather than disappointment. He told the young players that they are “young heroes” who brought honor and prestige to their country and wrote a golden chapter in Somali football history that all Somalis can be proud of. Speaker Abdulkadir praised the team’s skill, courage, unity, and love for the blue flag on the field. He expressed optimism that the players will achieve greater success in regional, continental, and international competitions in the future, and that they will make Somalia proud on football pitches around the world. A Final That Deserved More The Speaker’s recognition was not merely a consolation prize. As reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the final , Somalia matched Uganda, the tournament’s most dominant side, for 90 minutes and created the better chances in the second half. The Ocean Stars defended with discipline, pressed intelligently in midfield, and repeatedly broke forward with purpose. In the final 10 minutes, Somalia created three clear-cut opportunities against a defense that had conceded just once in three matches. A well-worked attack in the 85th minute ended in the hands of the Ugandan goalkeeper. Another opportunity went begging in the 88th. And in the 94th minute, Mahmoud Osman, the team’s standout forward, missed a golden chance to win the match. The final whistle sent the teams to penalties, where Uganda prevailed. The loss was painful, but the performance was not. As FTL Somalia noted, “the players showed character, tactical discipline, and a refusal to be overawed by an opponent that had demolished every other team in the tournament.” A Call for Private Sector Investment The Speaker used the occasion to issue a direct appeal to large companies and financially capable businesses, urging them to make real investments in Somali sports. He argued that such investment is necessary to improve the quality of national teams, nurture youth talent, and build a better future for Somali football. The call reflects a recognition that the national team’s achievements, however impressive, have been built on limited resources. The Somali Football Federation has relied on modest budgets, diaspora support, and the commitment of volunteers. The Under-17 team’s success in reaching the continental tournament in Morocco 2027 has generated goodwill and attention, but translating that into sustained infrastructure and coaching capacity requires funding that the government alone cannot provide. The meeting was attended by the First Deputy Speaker, the Secretary General of the House of the People, ministers including the Minister of Youth and Sports, members of parliament, officials from the Somali Football Federation, and other dignitaries. A Campaign That Captured the Nation The Under-17 team’s campaign in Kigali captured the imagination of Somalis at home and abroad. The Ocean Stars drew 1-1 with defending champions Tanzania in their opener, defeated Kenya 2-0, and advanced via a toss before overcoming hosts Rwanda on penalties in the semi-final, as reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the semi-final . Mahmoud Osman, the team’s standout forward, was among the players celebrated by the Speaker. His equalizer against Rwanda and his goals against Kenya made him one of the tournament’s most talked-about performers, as detailed in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the team’s campaign . The team’s qualification for the 2027 U-17 Africa Cup of Nations in Morocco was the primary objective, and it was achieved. The final against Uganda was an opportunity for a regional trophy, but the broader accomplishment, reaching the continental stage for the second time in four years, is a significant milestone for a country whose football infrastructure has been rebuilt from the ruins of conflict. Critical Note The Speaker’s recognition of the Under-17 team is a welcome gesture, and his call for private-sector investment in sports is a necessary one. The team’s success in Kigali was not the product of a well-funded system. It was the product of talent, determination, and the commitment of a coaching staff working with limited resources. If Somali football is to build on this achievement, it will need more than praise. It will need investment. The Speaker’s appeal to businesses is pragmatic. The government’s budget is stretched thin, and the Ministry of Youth and Sports has limited capacity to fund national teams, academies, and infrastructure. The private sector, which has prospered in Somalia’s telecommunications, banking, and logistics sectors, has the resources to make a difference. But it has not yet shown a sustained willingness to invest in sports at scale. The Speaker’s call is an invitation to change that. The deeper challenge is institutional. The Somali Football Federation has made progress in organizing regional leagues and youth tournaments, but it remains reliant on external support and volunteer energy. A national football academy, a properly funded coaching pipeline, and a domestic league that can retain talent are all necessary components of a sustainable system. The Under-17 team’s success is a proof of concept. Whether it becomes the foundation for something larger, or a fleeting moment of pride, will depend on whether the investment the Speaker called for actually materializes. Recommended Reading On ftlsomalia.com Heartbreak for Ocean Stars as Uganda Win CECAFA Final Shootout Somalia Beat Rwanda on Penalties to Reach CECAFA U-17 Final and Qualify for AFCON U17: Somalia’s Ocean Stars Set for Uganda Showdown After Osman’s Semi-Final Heroics Somalia Knock Out Kenya with 2-0 Win, Qualify for U-17 AFCON Qualifiers Semi-Finals Ismail Mohamed Shines as Somalia’s U-15 Cubs Win FIFA Opener Against Puerto Rico Facebook X Email WhatsApp LinkedIn Ismail Mohamed Shines as Somalia’s U-15 Cubs Win FIFA Opener Against Puerto Rico You may also like Sports Ismail Mohamed Shines as Somalia’s U-15 Cubs Win FIFA Opener Against Puerto Rico October 24, 2026 5 min read Sports Heartbreak for Ocean Stars as Uganda Win CECAFA Final Shootout October 19, 2026 6 min read Sports Ocean Stars Hold Firm Through 90 Minutes as CECAFA Final Goes to Shootout October 19, 2026 3 min read Latest News BECO, Mogadishu Power and Blue Sky Announce Rate Increase Amid 70% Fuel Spike Turkish Space Agency Delegation Visits Mogadishu and Warsheikh as Defence Cooperation Expands Puntland’s Deni Rejects Federal Election Process Somali U-17 Squad Celebrated at Parliament as Calls for Sports Investment Grow Hirshabelle Victory Celebrated in Mogadishu as Political Consensus Remains Elusive Oman’s Embassy Reopens in Mogadishu as Al-Umairi Presents Credentials Abiy Ahmed Calls Sea Access an Existential Issue for Ethiopia’s 130 Million People Somalia’s Danab Commandos Launch Operation Banooda, Kill Seven Al-Shabaab Militants Federal and Regional Leaders Congratulate Guudlaawe on Hirshabelle Re-election Mogadishu Hosts Uganda’s 64th Independence Anniversary After Landmark Trade Talks Facility for Talo and Leadership. Copyright © 2026. All Rights Reserved. ABOUT US | PRIVACY POLICY Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia",
